Idea

Root has HD = 0

Left child → HD - 1

Right child → HD + 1

Use a map<int, vector<int>> to store nodes for each vertical line.

Use a queue for BFS so nodes appear in level order.

Finally, read the map from leftmost HD to rightmost HD.

Time Complexity: O(n log n) (because of map)
Space Complexity: O(n)

C++ Code

***********************************************************************************************************************************************

class Solution {
  public:
    vector<vector<int>> verticalOrder(Node *root) {
        vector<vector<int>> ans;
        if(!root) return ans;
        
        map<int, vector<int>> mp;
        queue<pair<Node*, int>> q;
        
        q.push({root, 0});
        
        while(!q.empty()){
            auto temp = q.front();
            q.pop();
            
            Node* node = temp.first;
            int hd = temp.second;
            
            mp[hd].push_back(node->data);
            
            if(node->left) q.push({node->left, hd - 1});
            if(node->right) q.push({node->right, hd + 1});
        }
        
        for(auto &it : mp){
            ans.push_back(it.second);
        }
        
        return ans;
    }
};

*****************************************************************************************************************************************************************


Key Concept

Example tree:

        1
      /   \
     2     3
    / \   / \
   4  5  6   7

Horizontal distances:

HD -2 : 4
HD -1 : 2
HD  0 : 1,5,6
HD  1 : 3
HD  2 : 7

Output

[[4], [2], [1,5,6], [3], [7]]

Quick trick to remember

Vertical traversal problems usually combine:

BFS

Horizontal Distance

Map for ordering.
